<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- The rest of this app's Info.plist is generated from the
	     INFOPLIST_KEY_* build settings. What lives here is the three keys
	     that make the journal a folder the user owns rather than app storage
	     they cannot see — together they are what "the files are the journal"
	     (ADR 0001) looks like from the Files app — and, at the end, the two
	     sentences the system says on Aujour's behalf when a Content Template
	     asks for the day's calendar.

	     The pair below publishes the app's own Documents folder under "On My
	     iPhone", which is where a journal starts when iCloud Drive is off
	     (ADR 0004). They are written out here rather than as build settings
	     because `INFOPLIST_KEY_UIFileSharingEnabled` is silently ignored —
	     not every Info.plist key has a build setting behind it, and a missing
	     one shows up as a folder nobody can find rather than as a build
	     error. -->
	<key>UIFileSharingEnabled</key>
	<true/>
	<key>LSSupportsOpeningDocumentsInPlace</key>
	<true/>

	<!-- And this one publishes Aujour's iCloud Drive folder, the default
	     Journal Root: IsDocumentScopePublic puts the container's Documents
	     folder in the Files app under iCloud Drive › Aujour, and
	     SupportedFolderLevels = Any lets the Path Template nest entries in
	     YYYY/MM subfolders there.

	     Apple only re-reads this when the app's version changes, so a change
	     here needs MARKETING_VERSION or CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION bumped with
	     it, or the folder keeps its old visibility. -->
	<key>NSUbiquitousContainers</key>
	<dict>
		<key>iCloud.com.julesseguin.aujour</key>
		<dict>
			<key>NSUbiquitousContainerIsDocumentScopePublic</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSUbiquitousContainerName</key>
			<string>Aujour</string>
			<key>NSUbiquitousContainerSupportedFolderLevels</key>
			<string>Any</string>
		</dict>
	</dict>

	<!-- What the system alert says when {{events}} or {{reminders}} is in
	     somebody's Content Template. Written out here rather than as build
	     settings for the same reason as the file-sharing pair at the top: a
	     usage description that does not reach the bundle is a crash the
	     moment access is asked for, and there is no build error to see it
	     coming.

	     Full access, and it is the read half that is wanted: Aujour never
	     writes an event or a reminder — it copies the day's into the entry
	     being spawned, as markdown, and from then on they are words in the
	     user's file like any other. -->
	<key>NSCalendarsFullAccessUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Aujour fills in {{events}} with the day's calendar events when it starts a new entry. Your calendar is only ever read, and what it says goes no further than the markdown file for that day.</string>
	<key>NSRemindersFullAccessUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Aujour fills in {{reminders}} with the day's reminders when it starts a new entry. Your reminders are only ever read, and what they say goes no further than the markdown file for that day.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
